<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Debug-only policy. Overrides src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml
    in debug builds via Android manifest merger (debug source set wins).

    Policy: cleartext HTTP is PERMITTED for every host in debug builds. This
    is the standard Google-recommended setup for development against a local
    backend over plain HTTP (configuration is replaced by HTTPS/TLS pinning
    in release builds, which use src/main/res/xml/network_security_config.xml
    with base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false").

    Why global instead of a domain allow-list:
      - Dev devices move across networks (office WiFi, hotspot, emulator on
        different host). Hard-coding a small IP allow-list caused the login
        to silently fail with "No se pudo conectar al servidor" the moment
        the dev backend IP changed (192.168.2.10, 10.0.2.2, 192.168.x.x).
      - Debug binaries are NEVER shipped to end users; the security cost is
        zero. Release stays locked down via src/main.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
